---
/**
 * "On This Page" — a rail's in-page link list.
 *
 * ── PROMOTED ON ITS THIRD CONSUMER, WHICH IS ONE LATER THAN THE RULE ─────────
 *
 * TopicList's header named the condition and this is it: "if a THIRD dense link list
 * appears, the answer is to promote the treatment, not to write it a third time." The
 * article rail and the Reviews rail had both written it, and the `page` template was
 * about to be the third.
 *
 * The delay had already cost something measurable. The two existing copies had DRIFTED:
 * the article rail opted out of the wipe-in underline and restated
 * `text-decoration-thickness`, and Reviews did neither — so rail links animated the wipe
 * on one page and not the other, from the same design. Nobody chose that. Promoting it
 * is what makes "the rail behaves the same wherever it appears" (Andy, 2026-09-07) a
 * fact about one file rather than a convention three files are trusted to keep.
 *
 * ── WHAT IT OWNS, AND THE ONE ARGUABLE INCLUSION ─────────────────────────────
 *
 * The markup, the type, the spacing ramp and the link treatment — everything the three
 * pages said identically.
 *
 * It also owns its VISIBILITY, which is the arguable one, because this project's rule is
 * that placement belongs to the caller — TopicList sets no `grid-column` and says so.
 * `display` is not `grid-column`, and all three consumers hide this below md for the same
 * reason: the mobile boards draw no rail on any of them. Leaving it out would have made
 * the fourth copy of a rule that has never varied. If a page ever wants it on a phone,
 * that is the moment to hand the decision back.
 *
 * WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN IS STICKINESS. That is a property of the rail's wrapper, not of
 * this list, and it genuinely varies: the article pins two groups together in a
 * `.rail-sticky` child, Reviews pins the aside itself, and the `page` template pins this
 * while letting the hero above it scroll away. Three behaviours, one mechanism, none of
 * them this component's business.
 *
 * ── THE HEADING IS PINNED AND THE ID IS NOT ──────────────────────────────────
 *
 * "On This Page" is hardcoded, exactly as TopicList hardcodes "Topics": all three
 * consumers use it, and a field that does not vary should not be a prop.
 *
 * `id` IS required, and for TopicList's reason rather than for variation — every caller
 * passes the same string today. It has to be namespaced against the page's own body
 * heading ids, which are slugified from heading text, so an h2 titled "On This Page"
 * would collide. Requiring it keeps that reasoning at the call site instead of burying a
 * default that is only safe by luck.
 */
import type {HTMLAttributes} from 'astro/types'

interface Props extends HTMLAttributes<'nav'> {
  /** One entry per target: a same-page fragment id and the label to show for it. */
  items: {id: string; label: string}[]
  /**
   * The heading's id, which `aria-labelledby` also points at. The CALLER supplies it
   * because it has to be namespaced against that page's own body heading ids — see the
   * note above.
   */
  id: string
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the root, which is what lets a page place this. Astro passes a
     parent's scope attribute down as an ordinary prop and it reaches the DOM only if
     the component spreads rest — a component that does not forward silently cannot be
     styled by its caller. The house pattern; PageHeader's header records the bug. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {items, id, class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props
---

<nav class:list={['rail-nav', className]} aria-labelledby={id} {...rest}>
  {
    /*
    An <h3> rather than an <h2> on every consumer. This sits inside a rail that is
    tangential to the page's content, so its level is not carrying page structure, and
    the boards draw it at the same size as the h3 role.
  */
  }
  <h3 {id}>On This Page</h3>
  <ul role="list">
    {
      items.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</nav>

<style>
  /*
   * ── ABSENT BELOW md, NOT COLLAPSED ─────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * `display: none` rather than a visual hide, because the design says this is not
   * AVAILABLE on a phone: it should be out of the accessibility tree too. Every mobile
   * board agrees — "Insight Detail — Mobile" drops it while keeping Topics,
   * "Reviews — Mobile" has no rail at all, and "Consulting — Mobile" relocates the hero
   * and drops this.
   *
   * Mobile-first, per tokens.css: an engine that never evaluates the container query has
   * to be left with the phone layout. The cost of writing it this way round is that md
   * has to say `display: block` rather than unset anything.
   */
  .rail-nav {
    display: none;
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .rail-nav {
      display: block;
    }

    /* base.css keeps the UA's list indent for prose; a nav is not prose. */
    .rail-nav ul {
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
    }

    /* The ramp from base.css restated for a context it does not reach: 16 from a
       heading to its list. `heading-close` by name, not by value. */
    .rail-nav h3 + ul {
      margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    }

    /*
     * ── THE ITEM GAP IS ASKED FOR, NOT INHERITED ─────────────────────────────
     *
     * It used to arrive from base.css's global `li + li`, and this list was the one
     * place in the build genuinely relying on that rule. Removing it (2026-09-02)
     * collapsed these entries against each other — visible immediately on any article
     * with multi-line headings, and caught on screen rather than by the grep that found
     * the rule's eight cancellations. A list that depends on a global by saying nothing
     * is invisible to a search for overrides.
     *
     * `rhythm-item`, which is what it was getting and what TopicList's stacked form
     * asks for. That parity is a requirement rather than a coincidence: on an article
     * this list and Topics sit one above the other, and any difference between them
     * reads as a mistake.
     */
    .rail-nav li + li {
      margin-top: var(--rhythm-item);
    }
  }

  /* ── Links ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * OPTS OUT OF THE WIPE-IN UNDERLINE, which base.css's `nav` role provides for with
   * exactly this escape hatch. The wipe is a chrome gesture for the masthead and the
   * footer; a dense list of links animating one by one under a moving pointer is noise
   * rather than affordance. DESIGN.md → The rail.
   *
   * THE THICKNESS HAS TO BE RESTATED, and that is the easy one to miss. base.css gives
   * every anchor `text-decoration-thickness: 0.06em`, then `nav :is(a, button)` resets it
   * with the `text-decoration` SHORTHAND — which includes `-thickness`. Without the
   * declaration below the hover underline returns at the UA's `auto` weight, so two
   * underlines in one viewport read as two systems. `text-underline-offset` is NOT in the
   * shorthand and survives on its own.
   *
   * THE LINE IS DECLARED AT REST AND ONLY ITS COLOR MOVES, because
   * `text-decoration-line` is a discrete keyword that cannot animate from `none` — it
   * appeared instantly while everything else on the page eased.
   *
   * OUTSIDE THE md QUERY, unlike the spacing above: these are not layout, and scoping
   * them to a breakpoint would be a rule that happens to be unreachable rather than a
   * rule that means something.
   *
   * The hover COLOR is left to base.css's global `a:hover`. A rail sits on the page
   * ground, where `link-hover` is right and the underline is not the only signal.
   *
   * This is now the ONLY copy of this treatment for rail navs. TopicList still states
   * its own, and that is not a fourth copy waiting to be merged — it is a different
   * component with its own container query, and merging the two would couple them.
   */
  .rail-nav a::after {
    content: none;
  }

  .rail-nav a {
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 0.06em;
    text-decoration-color: transparent;
    transition:
      color 0.15s,
      text-decoration-color 0.15s;
  }

  .rail-nav a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }
</style>
